  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY THROUGH SUNDAY/... \nWest southwesterly drier flow aloft will spread over the rest of\nArizona by Tuesday with a brief time period of anticyclonic flow \naloft. The drier air aloft is expected to mix into the boundary \nlayer by Tuesday afternoon dropping surface dew points into the \n40s across southeast California to 55-60 degrees in the Phoenix \narea. Although we can't rule out a few isolated showers or weak \nthunderstorms Tuesday afternoon over the more favored higher \nterrain locations, the chances are only around 10-15%. \nTemperatures will remain below normal across south-central and \neastern Arizona with lower desert highs at around 100 degrees, \nwhile the drier western lower deserts should reach closer to 105 \ndegrees. \n\nThe drying aloft will continue to a lesser degree into Wednesday\nas low level mixing ratios drop to 5-6 g/kg in southeast\nCalifornia to 7-9 g/kg across much of Arizona. Chances for any\nshowers and storms should be similar or even less than Tuesday's\nchances. Temperatures should also warm another couple of degrees\nunder sunny to mostly sunny skies.\n\nForecast concern is increasing for the potential of tropical \nmoisture surging northward into the region as early as Thursday \nnight into Friday, but more so for the coming weekend. NHC shows a\ntropical disturbance currently near 10N and 107W with a 90% \nchance of cyclone formation this week and likely tracking \nnorthward off the coast of Baja by Friday into the weekend. There \nis still a lot of uncertainty with this potential tropical \nsystem, but model guidance has been coming to a bit better \nconsensus the last several model runs. Roughly 90% of the GEFS and\nEPS members now show PWATs increasing to 1.8-2.2\" over portions of \nour area by Friday or Saturday. Given this likely increase in \nmoisture, PoPs have gone up starting Friday and continue through \nthe weekend. It will likely be at least 2 or 3 more days until we \nhave a good idea on what would likely happen late this week. \nGetting tropical moisture into our region is definitely not \nunusual, but many times when it happens we struggle to build \ninstability due to extensive cloudiness and poor lapse rates. \nHowever, it seems quite likely a Pacific trough will be situated \nalong or just off the West Coast which may provide for enough \nupper level forced ascent to overcome the likely lackluster \ninstability. We will continue to watch this throughout the week as\nit very well could lead to another round of heavy rainfall at \nsome point.",
